---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Is TLS 1.3 backward compatible?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "It has a compatibility mode so middleboxes tolerate it, but 1.3-only servers drop ancient clients by design."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is a cipher suite?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The negotiated recipe: key exchange, bulk encryption, and hash algorithms. Modern suites are all forward-secret."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How do I check my server's TLS?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "SSL Labs Server Test grades protocol and cipher configuration, or openssl s_client with -tls1_2/-tls1_3 flags."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Does DNSSEC affect TLS?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "No: different layers. DNSSEC authenticates addresses; TLS authenticates and encrypts the session."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why did old TLS die?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "POODLE, BEAST, and downgrade attacks against design flaws that cannot be patched, only retired."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="TLS Versions Explained: 1.0 to 1.3 | PromptDNS"
  description="TLS versions explained: what changed from SSL 3.0 and TLS 1.0 through TLS 1.3, which to disable, and how to test your server."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ssl-tls/" class="hover:text-accent">SSL/TLS Guides</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">TLS Versions</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">TLS Versions Explained</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Every TLS version is a security generation. SSL 3.0 and TLS 1.0/1.1 are broken and retired; TLS 1.2 is the workhorse; TLS 1.3 is the modern protocol: faster, simpler, and more secure. Your server should offer 1.2 and 1.3, and nothing else.</p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">The versions</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60"><table class="pdns-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">SSL 3.0</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Dead (POODLE)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Retired 2015; disable everywhere</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">TLS 1.0</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Dead (BEAST, weak ciphers)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Retired 2020/2021 by browsers</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">TLS 1.1</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Dead (weak integrity)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Retired 2020/2021 by browsers</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">TLS 1.2</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Workhorse</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Secure with modern cipher suites; still widely required</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">TLS 1.3</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Modern</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Faster handshake (1-RTT), removed legacy ciphers, forward secrecy mandatory</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Why 1.3 matters</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li>One round-trip handshake instead of two: measurably faster connections.</li><li>Removed every legacy cipher, compression, and renegotiation: a smaller attack surface.</li><li>Forward secrecy on every connection: past traffic stays past even if keys leak later.</li><li>0-RTT resumption exists but carries replay caveats: use for GETs only.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Server configuration</h2>
      <p>Enable TLS 1.2 + 1.3, disable everything older, and prefer modern cipher suites (Cloudflare and major CDNs do this by default). Test with SSL Labs or <code>openssl s_client -tls1_2 -connect host:443</code>. PCI DSS and every modern compliance framework require disabling 1.0/1.1.</p>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is TLS 1.3 backward compatible?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">It has a compatibility mode so middleboxes tolerate it, but 1.3-only servers drop ancient clients by design.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is a cipher suite?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The negotiated recipe: key exchange, bulk encryption, and hash algorithms. Modern suites are all forward-secret.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How do I check my server's TLS?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">SSL Labs Server Test grades protocol and cipher configuration, or openssl s_client with -tls1_2/-tls1_3 flags.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does DNSSEC affect TLS?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: different layers. DNSSEC authenticates addresses; TLS authenticates and encrypts the session.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why did old TLS die?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">POODLE, BEAST, and downgrade attacks against design flaws that cannot be patched, only retired.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/what-is-ssl-tls/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">What is SSL/TLS →</a></li><li><a href="/hsts-explained/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">HSTS explained →</a></li><li><a href="/ssl-certificate-errors/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SSL errors →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
